{"type":"message","id":"fc2fd51d","parentId":"d038850c","timestamp":"2026-05-10T17:07:30.527Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_01VcAGbGbWN4A5mit1po1q8v","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Trail Code-Review SSE Stream\n\nTail a Trail's code review (discussion comments) in near-real time over Server-Sent Events.\n\n> Naming note: \"code review\" here refers to the live discussion thread on a Trail — the back-and-forth between author and reviewers. It is distinct from the structured `POST /review` / `GET /reviews` endpoints that record formal approve / request-changes verdicts.\n\n- **Endpoint**: `GET /api/v1/trails/:host/:owner/:repo/:number/code-review/stream`\n- **Auth**: Same as the rest of `/api/v1/trails/*` — Bearer token (`ent_…` API token sent via `Authorization: Bearer …`) **and** `users.is_admin = 1` (`requireAdmin`). Non-admins receive `403`.\n- **Content type**: `text/event-stream`.\n- **Implementation**: `api/src/routes/trails.ts` (`code_review_stream` route).\n\nThis document is the source of truth for client implementations (CLI, frontend). The OpenAPI description for the route mirrors this content.\n\n## Why polling SSE (and not push)?\n\nThe current implementation polls `repo_trails.getById` every ~1s and emits diffs. This is intentionally simple and has known scaling limits (one DB query per second per connected client). The long-term plan is a Durable Object that owns the subscription and broadcasts to N subscribers. Until then, the per-isolate concurrency caps below keep a single Worker from being overwhelmed.\n\n## Lifecycle\n\n```\nclient connect\n  ├── 200 OK, Content-Type: text/event-stream\n  ├── event: ready                       ← always first\n  ├── event: comment   (× N)             ← initial replay (suppressed in resume mode)\n  ├── …\n  ├── event: comment | comment_deleted   ← as the trail changes\n  ├── : ping                              ← keepalive every ~15s of idle time\n  └── one of:\n      ├── event: deleted     → trail row was deleted; stream closes\n      ├── event: reconnect   → max stream duration reached; client should reconnect\n      └── event: error       → unrecoverable server-side error\n```\n\nThe server caps each connection at **~50 seconds** to stay within Cloudflare Workers request limits. Clients must be prepared to reconnect — either via the browser `EventSource` auto-reconnect (which automatically resends the last `id:` as `Last-Event-ID`) or by re-issuing the request manually.\n\n## Resume semantics\n\nPass either:\n\n- `Last-Event-ID: <id>` HTTP header, **or**\n- `?replay=false` query parameter\n\n…to skip the initial replay. The server seeds its dedup state with the current trail discussion and only emits **changes from this point forward**.\n\n> **Note**: `Last-Event-ID` is currently used as a binary \"skip replay\" flag. The server does not parse the value — it does not need to, because the dedup map is rebuilt from current state on every connect. This means: if a comment's content was edited and reverted while you were disconnected, you will not see those events on reconnect. For threaded review comments this is acceptable; if you need exactly-once event delivery, switch to the (future) DO-backed implementation.\n\n## Event types\n\nAll named events carry a JSON `data:` payload. Only `ready`, `comment`, and `comment_deleted` include an `id:` line (those are the events a client may want to resume from). The terminal-ish events `reconnect`, `deleted`, and `error` are sent without an `id:` — on reconnect, clients should send the last `id:` they saw from a `ready`/`comment`/`comment_deleted` frame. The `: ping` keepalive is a bare SSE comment with no event/data/id.\n\n### `ready`\n\nEmitted exactly once, immediately after connect.\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"repo\": \"acme/web\",\n  \"trailNumber\": 42,\n  \"updatedAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\n  \"commentCount\": 1,\n  \"resumed\": false, // true when Last-Event-ID or ?replay=false suppressed replay\n}\n```\n\n`id`: `<updatedAtMs>:ready`.\n\n### `comment`\n\nEmitted for each existing comment during initial replay (unless suppressed) and whenever a comment is added or edited.\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"repo\": \"acme/web\",\n  \"trailNumber\": 42,\n  \"updatedAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:01:40Z\",\n  \"comment\": {\n    /* full TrailComment, including nested replies */\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`id`: `<updatedAtMs>:<commentId>`. Unchanged comments are not re-emitted on subsequent polls (dedup is signature-based: `JSON.stringify(comment)`).\n\n### `comment_deleted`\n\nEmitted when a comment present in a previous frame is no longer in the trail's discussion.\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"repo\": \"acme/web\",\n  \"trailNumber\": 42,\n  \"updatedAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:02:00Z\",\n  \"commentId\": \"c2\",\n}\n```\n\n`id`: `<updatedAtMs>:<commentId>:deleted`.\n\n### `reconnect`\n\nEmitted just before the server closes the stream because the max stream duration was reached. No `id:` line. Reconnect immediately and pass the most recent `id:` line you saw (from a `ready`/`comment`/`comment_deleted` frame) as `Last-Event-ID` (or use `?replay=false`). Today the only `reason` is `max_duration`.\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"repo\": \"acme/web\",\n  \"trailNumber\": 42,\n  \"reason\": \"max_duration\",\n}\n```\n\n### `deleted`\n\nEmitted when the underlying Trail row is deleted while you're connected. No `id:` line. The stream closes immediately after this event. Clients should not auto-reconnect.\n\n```jsonc\n{ \"repo\": \"acme/web\", \"trailNumber\": 42 }\n```\n\n### `error`\n\nEmitted when the polling loop hits an unrecoverable server-side error (e.g. DB outage). No `id:` line. The stream closes immediately. The `message` is intentionally generic — the underlying error (DB query fragment, connection details, etc.) is recorded server-side via OTEL spans and the structured close log line, never sent to the client. Clients should treat `error` as \"reconnect\".\n\n```jsonc\n{ \"message\": \"Stream error — please reconnect\" }\n```\n\n### `: ping`\n\nA bare SSE comment line (`: ping <epochMs>\\n\\n`). Sent every ~15 seconds of idle time so intermediaries don't close the connection. No `event:` or `data:`. Clients should ignore it.\n\n## Concurrency limits\n\nPer Worker isolate (not global):\n\n- **5** concurrent streams per user.\n- **1** concurrent stream per (user, trail).\n\nExcess connections receive `429 Too Many Requests` with `{ \"error\": \"<reason>\" }`. These limits are a safeguard against runaway clients and assume a typical client only needs one tail per trail. The right long-term fix is fan-out via a Durable Object so a hot trail with N viewers costs one DB poll, not N.\n\n## Performance characteristics\n\n- **Polling cadence**: ~1s. Latency floor for new events is therefore ~1s.\n- **Per-connection cost**: 1 PK lookup on `repo_trails` per second + 1 small JSON parse + one diff over the comment array.\n- **Skipped work**: when `repo_trails.updated_at` is unchanged, the discussion JSON is not re-parsed.\n- **Max duration**: 50 seconds per connection.\n- **Keepalive**: 15 seconds.\n- **Memory**: O(comment count) per connection (signature dedup map).\n\n## Observability\n\nEvery stream emits structured logs and OTEL span attributes for ops:\n\n- **Open**: `console.log` line `[trail-code-review-stream] opened repo=<full> trail=<n> user=<id> resumed=<bool>` plus a `trail.code_review_stream.opened` span event with the same fields.\n- **Close**: `console.log` (or `console.error` for the `error` reason) `[trail-code-review-stream] closed reason=<r> repo=<full> trail=<n> user=<id> duration_ms=<ms> polls=<n> events=<n>` plus a `trail.code_review_stream.closed` span event. `reason` is one of `aborted` (client disconnect), `max_duration`, `deleted`, `error`, `completed`. On `error`, the underlying exception is also fed through `recordSpanError` and surfaces as a span error status — it is **not** included in the wire payload.\n- **Span attributes** (set on the active request span): `trail.stream.repo`, `trail.stream.trail_number`, `trail.stream.user_id`, `trail.stream.duration_ms`, `trail.stream.polls_run`, `trail.stream.events_sent`, `trail.stream.close_reason`, `trail.stream.resumed`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Unit tests: `api/src/routes/trails.test.ts` (100 cases total, ~14 dedicated to the SSE stream).\n- Registry tests: `api/src/lib/trail-code-review-stream-registry.test.ts` (5 cases).\n- Integration test (real MySQL): `api/test/trail-code-review-stream.test.ts` (3 cases, incl. a real-DB write → wire round-trip). Run with `pnpm run api:test` after `mise run api-db-start`.\n\n## Client implementation notes\n\n- Browsers: `EventSource` works out of the box, but cannot set `Authorization`. Use a fetch-based polyfill or move auth into a cookie / proxy.\n- Node / CLI: native `EventSource` (Node 22+) does not consistently propagate `Last-Event-ID` on reconnect — prefer the `eventsource` npm package, or hand-roll a fetch loop that:\n  1. On `event: reconnect`, reconnect immediately with no backoff.\n  2. On network error, exponential backoff starting at 500ms.\n  3. Send the most recently observed `id:` line as `Last-Event-ID`.\n- The wire payload's `comment` object matches the `TrailComment` TypeScript type in `api/src/types.ts`. Treat the rest of the envelope as the shapes documented above.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1778432850527}}
